{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/1496206-social-merits-status-and-residential-area","id":1496206,"title":"Social merits (status and Residential area)","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Werewolf: The Forsaken"],"posts":[{"date":"2022-11-04T11:10:11-07:00","id":1496206,"title":"Social merits (status and Residential area)","content":"So we are playing a game where we can play any race from any chronicles game. And I'm playing a werewolf Elodoth Bone Shadow. In this game we're all living in an isolated island community. The community has quickly growing population, but it is still a very small island. My character will be effectively the Town elder/ an important leader of the community.<br /> <br /> Then this leads me to two questions about merits, particularly the Status merit and the Residential Area merit community merit from werewolf. Obviously my character will have quite a bit of status in the town as he is very respected and very well liked by most people in the island. But the iron itself isn't exactly any kind of major player. And I'm not sure how much people outside of the island would know who he i. But obviously he has a hole island community/ town which is who do know who he is, so would have fair amount of points in the Residential Area merit as well. <br /> <br /> But I'm struggling to figure out how I would allocate these points, and exactly what they would mean per point. Can anyone give any advice with this?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10150-bob275","name":"bob275","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"29"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-11-04T12:43:59-07:00","id":1496217,"title":"","content":"Talk to your ST.<br /> <br /> Both Merits have a very subjective value (on purpose) based on the scope of your game. If the entire game is going to take place on the island, high Status there is a big deal even if that level of Status wouldn't be justified in a game that, say, including significant dealings with a nearby major city on the mainland. These are purposefully abstract so they can be scaled with the scope of the game. In a small isolated town, any business owner - even the local gas station owner - could have Status 1 or 2 because they've got a decent amount of clout and respect In a big city being the owner of one of the few remaining independent gas stations still wouldn't justify Status 1 by itself because... that doesn't make you someone important.<br /> <br /> As for how many dots to put in each? Mostly how many you can afford considering anything else you want to snag as well. If you're struggling with dots, having Status 2 because your position isn't official, and between the mayor, chief of police, and a large influx of new residents you're just not as socially powerful as you might have been before can work even if in theory a town elder could easily justify Status 5 in the same scenario if you want to put in the dots for it.<br /> <br /> There's also just a stylistic difference between the two to consider. Status implies official - even if informal - power. Taking high Status even for an informal position like town elder, implies you're making a character that is treated as a local authority figure. People are going to come to you for advice, to resolve disputes, to weigh in on public debates, etc. It's a character who's words have power, and will need to act like it. Residential Area is more of a &quot;soft power&quot; approach where your PC is just the old friendly face everyone recognizes and socializes with. You talk people up about having a bake sale to raise money for something, or organize a potluck to help take care of someone that just lost their job so they feel supported by their community (and will then feel less constrained about doing you a favor). Etc.<br /> <br /> Nothing is wrong with taking both, but with limited creation dots, it's probably best to consider what you envision here regarding both how other people see your character, and how they approach exercising social power.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}